1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,060
Your next favourite skincare ingredient may
have been invented by bacteria

2
00:00:04,060 --> 00:00:05,371
trying not to die.

3
00:00:05,753 --> 00:00:06,804
Not snail mucin.

4
00:00:06,804 --> 00:00:08,242
Not salmon DNA.

5
00:00:08,242 --> 00:00:11,212
And not another form
of hyaluronic acid.

6
00:00:11,362 --> 00:00:16,062
This tiny molecule helps microorganisms survive
places so salty and so

7
00:00:16,242 --> 00:00:20,022
dry that an ordinary cell would
lose its water and stop functioning.

8
00:00:20,172 --> 00:00:22,482
And it is now turning up,

9
00:00:22,482 --> 00:00:26,044
quietly, in Korean serums,
creams and sunscreens.

10
00:00:26,044 --> 00:00:29,304
It is called ectoin, and the
real science is stranger than

11
00:00:29,304 --> 00:00:30,534
the marketing.

12
00:00:30,684 --> 00:00:33,488
Ectoin is what researchers
call an extremolyte.

13
00:00:33,488 --> 00:00:34,966
That sounds like a superhero.

14
00:00:34,966 --> 00:00:40,016
It just means a protective molecule
made by microbes living somewhere extreme.

15
00:00:40,266 --> 00:00:43,576
The bacterium we get most of
it from has a name.

16
00:00:43,576 --> 00:00:45,551
Halomonas elongata.

17
00:00:45,551 --> 00:00:46,633
It lives in salt.

18
00:00:46,633 --> 00:00:48,873
When the salt outside gets punishing,

19
00:00:48,873 --> 00:00:50,663
it floods itself with ectoin.

20
00:00:50,913 --> 00:00:51,913
Why?

21
00:00:51,933 --> 00:00:53,360
Because salt pulls water
out of cells.

22
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:56,191
Lose enough water and proteins
start to lose their shape,

23
00:00:56,551 --> 00:00:59,881
membranes get unstable, and
the cell stops working.

24
00:01:00,131 --> 00:01:03,892
Ectoin keeps the space around
those fragile structures survivable.

25
00:01:03,892 --> 00:01:06,755
Which led skincare researchers
to an obvious question.

26
00:01:06,755 --> 00:01:09,555
Could it do the same
for stressed human skin?

27
00:01:10,105 --> 00:01:13,748
Here is where ectoin stops
behaving like a normal moisturiser.

28
00:01:13,748 --> 00:01:17,464
A familiar humectant, glycerin, hyaluronic acid,

29
00:01:17,754 --> 00:01:19,227
attracts water and holds onto it.

30
00:01:19,227 --> 00:01:23,771
Ectoin seems to work on how water
arranges itself around proteins and membranes.

31
00:01:24,021 --> 00:01:26,808
Scientists call the idea preferential exclusion.

32
00:01:26,838 --> 00:01:27,991
The tidy version.

33
00:01:27,991 --> 00:01:31,197
Ectoin protects proteins by
staying off their surface,

34
00:01:31,197 --> 00:01:34,118
which helps the water
around them stay organised.

35
00:01:34,138 --> 00:01:36,467
The honest version is messier.

36
00:01:36,487 --> 00:01:41,159
In the simulations, ectoin also showed
some attraction to that surface.

37
00:01:41,159 --> 00:01:44,419
And how far it stays back
depends on how tightly the

38
00:01:44,419 --> 00:01:47,589
protein's own water is already arranged.

39
00:01:47,739 --> 00:01:50,609
Think of a celebrity inside
a ring of security.

40
00:01:50,629 --> 00:01:53,209
Ectoin is not hugging the celebrity.

41
00:01:53,269 --> 00:01:56,580
It hangs back, and helps
the ring hold its shape.

42
00:01:56,590 --> 00:02:01,510
Which is why calling it another
hydrating ingredient misses the interesting part.

43
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:04,970
Your outer skin barrier
is under constant load.

44
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,299
Dry air, cleansing, pollution,
sun, strong actives.

45
00:02:08,312 --> 00:02:10,908
When it struggles, water
escapes more easily,

46
00:02:10,981 --> 00:02:12,851
and skin can feel tight, rough,

47
00:02:12,851 --> 00:02:14,681
or unusually reactive.

48
00:02:14,831 --> 00:02:20,444
Lab research suggests ectoin can
help stabilise biological structures and

49
00:02:20,444 --> 00:02:24,197
improve hydration in that outer layer.

50
00:02:24,197 --> 00:02:26,913
One study found it
changed how keratin,

51
00:02:27,087 --> 00:02:29,987
the main protein in
our outer skin cells,

52
00:02:29,987 --> 00:02:31,564
behaves with water.

53
00:02:31,714 --> 00:02:35,135
Though in that same
study, in punishing dryness,

54
00:02:35,135 --> 00:02:39,525
below five humidity, one stress
measure actually got worse.

55
00:02:39,535 --> 00:02:41,701
It is not a force field.

56
00:02:41,911 --> 00:02:45,491
It is better understood as a
supporting ingredient that may help

57
00:02:45,771 --> 00:02:49,671
skin handle dehydration and
environmental stress more comfortably.

58
00:02:50,121 --> 00:02:54,301
So does it do
anything to actual people?

59
00:02:54,301 --> 00:02:56,681
In one randomised, double study,

60
00:02:56,681 --> 00:03:00,841
a women used a cream with
two ectoin against the same

61
00:03:00,841 --> 00:03:01,879
cream without it.

62
00:03:02,079 --> 00:03:03,946
On the study's own measure,

63
00:03:03,946 --> 00:03:09,041
the women themselves preferred the ectoin
version. That is a real result.

64
00:03:09,041 --> 00:03:10,727
And it is a preference,

65
00:03:10,727 --> 00:03:13,566
not a machine reading their skin.

66
00:03:13,716 --> 00:03:18,730
A second randomised study followed sixty
people with mild to moderate

67
00:03:18,730 --> 00:03:20,274
atopic dermatitis, eczema.

68
00:03:20,274 --> 00:03:23,556
Over four weeks, an ectoin cream
performed about as well as

69
00:03:23,603 --> 00:03:26,243
the barrier cream it
was tested against,

70
00:03:26,243 --> 00:03:28,768
and was well tolerated.

71
00:03:28,918 --> 00:03:29,918
Encouraging.

72
00:03:29,938 --> 00:03:31,893
Not proof that ectoin cures eczema,

73
00:03:32,130 --> 00:03:36,125
reverses ageing, or replaces
anything a dermatologist prescribes.

74
00:03:36,275 --> 00:03:38,697
And the evidence base
is genuinely small.

75
00:03:38,698 --> 00:03:44,691
Search PubMed today and you will
find twelve ectoin clinical trials. Twelve.

76
00:03:44,891 --> 00:03:50,006
Some of that research also comes from people
who sell the ingredient. Bitop, Merck, Kao. So.

77
00:03:50,006 --> 00:03:52,044
Promising supporting ingredient, yes.

78
00:03:52,054 --> 00:03:53,694
Miracle molecule, no.

79
00:03:54,144 --> 00:03:57,171
Ectoin is most interesting if
your skin runs dry,

80
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:00,642
sensitive, over cleansed, or irritated
by a strong routine.

81
00:04:00,664 --> 00:04:02,889
It sits comfortably alongside panthenol,

82
00:04:02,889 --> 00:04:05,106
glycerin, squalane and ceramides.

83
00:04:05,356 --> 00:04:09,027
But do not buy it off
the front of the bottle.

84
00:04:09,132 --> 00:04:10,384
Turn it around.

85
00:04:10,684 --> 00:04:12,884
Plenty of brands do
print a number.

86
00:04:13,484 --> 00:04:17,346
Paula's Choice says seven
The Ordinary says two.

87
00:04:17,546 --> 00:04:22,276
And Abib's Ectoin Panthenol eleven That
eleven is the two of

88
00:04:22,606 --> 00:04:23,692
them added together.

89
00:04:23,692 --> 00:04:27,901
On the ingredient list, panthenol
is second. Ectoin is eleventh.

90
00:04:27,901 --> 00:04:31,968
The big number on the front
is not always the ectoin number.

91
00:04:32,218 --> 00:04:35,028
The rest is judgement, not evidence.

92
00:04:35,028 --> 00:04:38,368
Is it fragrance free,
if fragrance bothers you?

93
00:04:38,368 --> 00:04:41,559
Does the formula carry
other useful moisturisers?

94
00:04:41,559 --> 00:04:44,829
One good ingredient cannot rescue
a badly built product.

95
00:04:45,279 --> 00:04:48,335
K did not invent ectoin.

96
00:04:48,335 --> 00:04:51,635
What Korean formulators are doing well
is pairing it with barrier

97
00:04:52,215 --> 00:04:54,425
ingredients in light, wearable textures.

98
00:04:54,675 --> 00:04:59,493
That fits the wider move away
from aggressive transformation and towards

99
00:04:59,493 --> 00:05:02,291
skin that simply stays comfortable.

100
00:05:02,291 --> 00:05:05,143
You will already find it
in Korean barrier serums,

101
00:05:05,222 --> 00:05:09,774
toners and sun products, often in
smaller type than the trend suggests.

102
00:05:10,274 --> 00:05:13,427
So next time you see
ectoin on an ingredient list,

103
00:05:13,647 --> 00:05:15,294
remember what you are looking at.

104
00:05:15,294 --> 00:05:18,456
A survival strategy, borrowed from bacteria.

105
00:05:18,456 --> 00:05:20,909
Not the new hyaluronic
acid. Not a miracle.

106
00:05:20,909 --> 00:05:24,270
A genuinely interesting supporting molecule.

107
00:05:24,770 --> 00:05:26,945
Here is the one
thing worth doing.

108
00:05:26,945 --> 00:05:31,095
Turn the bottle around and find
the actual percentage before you buy.

109
00:05:31,095 --> 00:05:34,963
And tell me, would you put
a bacteria made survival molecule

110
00:05:34,963 --> 00:05:36,645
on your face?
